Eating disorders
Young Adult & Adolecence
Intense fear of weight gain, Distorted body image, Dietary restriction/Binge-purge cycle

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-012
Reason for Visit: Acute presyncope, severe palpitations, and extreme lethargy in the context of prolonged dietary restriction and suspected purging.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient experiencing severe palpitations, generalized muscle weakness, and near-collapse upon standing, secondary to extreme caloric restriction and recurrent emesis. Urgent after-hours clinical assessment was critical to evaluate hemodynamic stability, perform an ECG to rule out fatal arrhythmias associated with severe electrolyte derangement (hypokalaemia), and determine if immediate emergency department transfer was required prior to the availability of routine clinical services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My heart is fluttering wildly in my chest, my legs feel like lead, and I felt like I was going to pass out when I tried to shower."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 48-hour history of progressively worsening palpitations and profound generalized weakness. The collateral history from the flatmate, combined with the patient's admission, reveals an intense fear of gaining weight and a severely distorted body image. The patient reports severe dietary restriction over the past three months, recently complicated by recurrent episodes of binge eating followed by inappropriate compensatory behaviours, specifically self-induced vomiting multiple times daily. The acute palpitations and presyncope prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; unable to stand without severe dizziness and tachycardia.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of sudden cardiac death secondary to electrolyte imbalance; flatmate is highly distressed and unable to manage the medical risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 88/55 (Supine) -> 70/40 (Standing) | HR: 48 (Supine) -> 110 (Standing) | Temp: 35.8 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 42 Kg
General: Patient appears markedly cachectic, pale, and lethargic, with noticeably sunken cheeks and delayed capillary refill.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular examination demonstrates profound orthostatic hypotension and significant positional tachycardia. A baseline resting state shows sinus bradycardia. Skin examination reveals fine downy body hair (lanugo) on the arms and back. Inspection of the hands demonstrates Russell's sign (calluses on the dorsal surface of the dominant hand). Oral examination reveals noticeable dental enamel erosion secondary to repeated emesis, alongside bilateral parotid gland hypertrophy.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty water bottles and diet pill containers visible; bathroom smells faintly of vomitus.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient admits to episodic over-the-counter laxative abuse; nil prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute symptomatic hypovolemia and suspected hypokalaemia secondary to severe Eating Disorder (Binge-Purge Subtype) - Status: Critical Exacerbation
Differentials:

Primary Cardiac Arrhythmia (e.g., SVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of resting profound bradycardia and the clear mechanical/metabolic etiology of the positional tachycardia.

Hyperthyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of hypothermia, bradycardia (supine), and profound cachexia linked directly to deliberate dietary restriction, rather than hypermetabolic state.

Gastrointestinal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit presence of the diagnostic tetrad: intense fear of weight gain, distorted body image, and clear behavioural cycles of restriction and purging.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Medical risk of fatal arrhythmia)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits critical signs of severe physiological compromise secondary to an eating disorder, including profound orthostatic hypotension, resting bradycardia, and symptomatic palpitations. These objective findings strongly suggest severe hypovolemia and an underlying electrolyte derangement, critically a hypokalaemia, hypochloraemia, and a metabolic alkalosis. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an immediate, unacceptable risk of sudden cardiac arrest or traumatic syncopal injury. The urgent home evaluation confirmed severe hemodynamic instability and mandated immediate escalation to the emergency department for intravenous fluid resuscitation, urgent electrolyte replacement, and continuous cardiac monitoring.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate escalation and transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000 called by clinician). Anticipated need for urgent serum electrolyte panel, 12-lead electrocardiogram, and supervised refeeding protocols.
Positive Behavioral Change: To safely manage the immediate physical danger prior to ambulance arrival, remain strictly supine with legs elevated, directly maintaining cerebral blood flow and preventing syncopal collapse. To begin the process of medical stabilization, consent to the ambulance transfer and initial blood tests, recognizing that correcting the immediate heart rhythm danger is required before any psychological therapy can commence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None. Immediate ED transfer.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Post-discharge GP review within 48 hours to coordinate multi-disciplinary eating disorder team involvement.

